# Code Style

Enforce consistent coding style for the Next.js 16.2.9 TypeScript application.

## TypeScript Conventions

### Formatting & Linting
- **Formatter:** Prettier (via ESLint integration)
- **Linter:** ESLint 9 with `eslint-config-next` (types + web-vitals)
- **Strict mode:** Enabled in tsconfig.json — no `any`, no implicit returns
- Run `pnpm lint` before committing

### React Patterns
- Prefer function components with hooks
- Use `@/*` path alias for imports (maps to `./src/*`)
- Server Components by default; add `"use client"` only when needed
- Keep components small — extract sub-components at ~100 lines

```typescript
// Good - typed props, path alias
import { SignalCard } from "@/components/signal-card";

interface ArticleListProps {
  signals: Signal[];
  onSelect: (id: string) => void;
}

export function ArticleList({ signals, onSelect }: ArticleListProps) {
  return (
    <ul>
      {signals.map((s) => (
        <SignalCard key={s.id} signal={s} onClick={() => onSelect(s.id)} />
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

### Naming
- Components: `PascalCase` (`SignalCard.tsx`)
- Utilities/hooks: `camelCase` (`use-signals.ts`, `format-date.ts`)
- Types: `PascalCase` with descriptive suffix (`SignalData`, `AnalysisResult`)
- Constants: `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` (`MAX_RETRIES`, `API_BASE_URL`)

### Error Handling
- Use typed error boundaries in React. Never swallow errors silently.

### Comments
- Write self-documenting code; comment only *why*, never *what*
- Use JSDoc for public APIs
- Add `TODO(name):` with a ticket reference for deferred work

### File Size
Keep files under 300 lines. Split when a file has multiple distinct responsibilities.
